          Start with your surface, not the brand. Blacktop gets an asphalt emulsion or filler sealer; concrete gets a penetrating repellent or an acrylic. Mixing those up is the most expensive mistake shoppers make in this aisle. For blacktop, Latex-ite Airport Grade is the best all-round pail most stores carry. For concrete, Foundation Armor SX5000 is the low-maintenance choice and BEHR Wet-Look is the one to buy if you want shine.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 5 Home Depot Driveway Sealers Worth Buying
          </h2>

          <p>
            These picks are based on manufacturer labels and data sheets, coverage and cure specifications, the product categories each formula is built for, and recurring patterns in owner feedback. Availability varies by store and season, so confirm stock on the product page before driving over.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="surface-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Surface First: Asphalt and Concrete Need Different Products
          </h2>

          <p>
            Home Depot shelves blacktop pails and concrete sealers in the same seasonal aisle, often on neighboring pallets, and the packaging does not always make the difference obvious. The rule is simple. If your driveway is black and slightly soft on a hot day, it is asphalt and needs an asphalt emulsion or filler sealer. If it is gray, hard and was poured in slabs with control joints, it is concrete and needs a concrete product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Asphalt sealers replace the binder that sun and oxygen strip from the surface, slow water intrusion, and restore color. Put one on concrete and you get a black coating that never bonds properly and peels in sheets. Concrete sealers either penetrate and repel water or form a clear acrylic film. Put one on asphalt and it does essentially nothing useful.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some products are sold as suitable for both surfaces. They can work as a light protective coat, but they rarely do either job as well as a dedicated product. For a driveway that matters to you, buy the sealer built for its surface, and if you have both, such as an asphalt driveway with a concrete apron, buy two products.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pavers are a third case. Interlocking pavers need a paver sealer that also stabilizes the joint sand, which is a different category altogether. Our <a href="/best-paver-sealer/">paver sealer guide</a> covers those.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price-tiers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Budget, Mid-Range or Premium: Matching the Tier to the Driveway
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every brand in the aisle sells roughly three tiers, and the right one depends on the condition of your asphalt rather than on how much you want to spend. Paying for a premium pail on a smooth, two-year-old surface wastes money on solids that have nothing to fill. Buying the cheapest pail for a gray, open-textured driveway wastes the whole weekend, because a thin coat soaks in and disappears.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at the surface in bright sun. If it is still dark, smooth and tight, with only a little fading, a budget or mid-range emulsion is all it needs, and a single careful coat may be enough. If it has turned gray, you can see individual stones standing proud, and water darkens it instead of beading, it has lost binder and needs a filler sealer in two coats. If it is rough, porous and has not been sealed in five years or more, that is where the premium polymer pails earn their price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Climate shifts the decision as well. In places with hard freezes and road salt, the flexibility from polymer modification is worth paying for, because a brittle film cracks with the pavement each winter. In mild climates with little freeze-thaw, the main enemy is ultraviolet light, and a mid-range filler sealer applied on schedule does the job at lower cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Think about cost per year rather than cost per pail. A premium coat that lasts three years can be cheaper over time than a budget coat redone every year, once you count your weekend and the tools. But that math only works if you actually do the prep. A premium pail laid over dust and oil fails as quickly as a cheap one. Our <a href="/how-often-should-you-seal-your-driveway/">resealing frequency guide</a> helps set a realistic schedule for your driveway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reading-labels" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Reading the Labels: What the Words on the Pail Mean
          </h2>

          <p>
            Blacktop pails use a small vocabulary, and knowing it saves money. A plain sealer or sealcoat is a thin asphalt emulsion: inexpensive, high coverage, light protection. A filler sealer carries mineral filler that fills worn texture and hairline cracks. Polymer-modified or rubberized means added polymers for flexibility, which helps in freeze-thaw climates. Those three tiers map roughly to the budget, mid and premium price points.
          </p>

          <p>
            Service-life numbers on the front of the pail are brand claims for ideal application, not guarantees for your driveway. Treat them as a ranking within one brand's line rather than a prediction. A pail labeled for a longer life will usually outlast a cheaper pail from the same maker, but heavy traffic, shade and skipped prep shorten every one of them.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Label term</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What it means</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Who it suits</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sealer / sealcoat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thin asphalt emulsion</td>
