                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Decorative or stamped concrete</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            On concrete, the key split is penetrating versus film-forming. Penetrating sealers vanish into the surface and protect from inside; film-formers add a visible coat with sheen. Our explainer on <a href="/oil-based-vs-water-based-driveway-sealer/">oil-based versus water-based sealers</a> covers the solvent question that sits on top of that choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, read the application conditions printed on the side. Minimum temperature, rain-free hours and time before driving are not suggestions. They are the conditions under which the coverage and lifespan on the front were achieved.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-many-pails" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Many Pails to Put on the Cart
          </h2>

          <p>
            Measure the paved area in square feet, length times width, adding the apron and any parking pad. Then divide by the label coverage for a single coat, and double it for two coats. Most blacktop filler sealers in 4.75-gallon pails list somewhere in the range of 250 to 400 square feet per pail, with thin emulsions covering more and heavy premium pails covering less.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Driveway size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approx. area</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Filler sealer, two coats</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Thin emulsion, two coats</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Single car, short</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">300 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 pails</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 pails</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two car, standard</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">600 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-5 pails</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-4 pails</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two car, long</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,000 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6-8 pails</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-6 pails</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Three car with turnaround</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,500 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">9-12 pails</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7-9 pails</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Use the low end of the label range for older, rough or porous asphalt, which drinks the first coat. Use the high end for smooth surfaces that were sealed within the last few years. Then add one spare pail. Unopened pails are generally returnable within the store's return window, so the spare costs you nothing if you do not use it, and it saves a mid-job store run with half the driveway wet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight matters too. A full pail of asphalt sealer weighs somewhere around 50 to 60 pounds, so six pails is the better part of 350 pounds. Use a flatbed cart, check your vehicle's payload, and consider curbside pickup if you are buying a large order.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cart-checklist" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Rest of the Cart: Tools and Prep Products
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealer is only half the purchase. The tools and prep products decide whether it lasts. At minimum you need a squeegee or a sealer brush with a long handle, a stiff push broom, a trim brush for edges, a drill-mounted mixing paddle, and painter's tape or plastic sheeting to protect the garage floor and walkways.
          </p>

          <p>
            For prep, add a driveway cleaner or degreaser, an oil-spot primer for any stains that stay tacky after scrubbing, and crack filler sized for your cracks: pourable filler for narrow cracks, rope filler for wider ones, and a cold patch for shallow holes. Our <a href="/best-driveway-crack-filler/">crack filler rankings</a> help you choose the right type.
          </p>

          <p>
            A leaf blower and a garden hose round out the job. If your driveway is dirty enough to need pressure washing, a surface cleaner attachment gives much more even results than a wand. Our <a href="/best-surface-cleaner-for-pressure-washer/">surface cleaner guide</a> lists attachments that fit common washers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, buy disposable gloves, old shoes you can throw out, and a box of rags. Asphalt sealer is difficult to remove from skin and nearly impossible to remove from clothing once dry, so plan on the gear being disposable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-options" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. In-Store, Pickup or Delivery: How to Buy Without the Hassle
          </h2>

          <p>
            Check online stock before you go. Product pages show availability at your local store, and sealer sells out quickly on the first warm weekends of spring. If the store shows only a few pails, buy online for in-store pickup so the order is held for you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some products in this guide, especially concrete penetrating sealers like Foundation Armor, are more often sold online and shipped to the store or the house. Order those a week ahead. Freight-heavy orders of many pails may qualify for delivery; compare the delivery fee against the time and fuel of hauling them yourself.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you seal driveways regularly, for a rental property or for neighbors, the Pro Xtra program can bring volume pricing and purchase tracking. For a single homeowner job, watch for spring seasonal promotions instead, which commonly cover outdoor maintenance products.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the receipt and leave spare pails sealed. Return policies can change, so check the current terms, but unopened sealer is generally the easiest item in this aisle to bring back.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="season" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. When Stock Peaks and When to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Driveway sealer is a seasonal product. In most of the country, stores fill the aisle in early spring, keep it full through summer, and let stock run down in fall. The widest selection and the best chance of finding the premium pails on the shelf come in April and May.
          </p>

          <p>
            That timing lines up with the sealing calendar in cooler climates only partly. Late spring and early fall are often the best sealing windows because temperatures are warm enough to cure but not so hot that the sealer flashes dry before it levels. Our guide to the <a href="/best-time-to-seal-driveway/">best time to seal a driveway</a> explains why mid-summer heat can cause problems too.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you plan to seal in September, buy in August. Fall clearance can bring discounts, but the products left are often the ones nobody wanted, and the pail count you need may not be there. Store sealer indoors, off the floor, and never let it freeze; a frozen emulsion separates and is ruined.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not stockpile across years. Most emulsions have a shelf life of about a year or two in a sealed pail, and settled filler in an old pail can be very hard to remix.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sealing-weekend" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. A Two-Day Plan for Sealing Day
          </h2>

          <p>
            Day one is preparation. Trim grass back from the edges, sweep and blow the surface, scrub oil spots, and pressure wash or scrub with cleaner. Fill cracks and patch holes, following the cure times on those products. Let everything dry until the end of the day and through the night.
          </p>

          <p>
            Day two is sealing. Start mid-morning once the dew has burned off. Tape the garage threshold, cut in the edges with a brush, then pour and spread in sections, working from the garage toward the street so you never trap yourself. Let the first coat dry, which in sun can take a few hours, and apply the second coat in the opposite direction.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE THAT SAVES MOST JOBS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Two thin coats, never one thick one.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Thick single coats skin over, stay soft underneath and crack or track. Two thin coats use the same product and cure harder. If you only have time for one coat, make it a thin, even one.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Then block the driveway. Keep cars off for a minimum of one full day and preferably two, adding time whenever nights are cool or the air is muggy. Our article on <a href="/how-long-for-driveway-sealer-to-dry/">drying times</a> breaks the wait down by condition, and our full <a href="/how-to-seal-a-driveway/">step-by-step sealing guide</a> covers technique in more depth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-not-to-seal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When No Pail From the Aisle Will Help
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealer protects good pavement. It does not repair bad pavement. If your asphalt has widespread alligator cracking, sunken areas, potholes or crumbling edges, the base underneath has failed, and a coat of sealer will look good for a few weeks before the same cracks reappear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Likewise, brand-new asphalt should not be sealed right away. It needs months to cure, and sealing too early can trap oils and leave the surface soft. Our guide on <a href="/when-should-you-seal-a-new-asphalt-driveway/">when to seal a new asphalt driveway</a> gives the timeline.
          </p>

          <p>
            On concrete, sealer will not stop structural cracking or fix heaved slabs, and it will not reverse scaling that has already happened. It slows further damage. If the surface is already flaking badly, talk to a concrete contractor about resurfacing before you spend money on sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everything else, a well-chosen pail and a careful weekend give real value. Our ranked <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">asphalt driveway sealer guide</a> covers brands beyond the Home Depot aisle, and our <a href="/driveway-sealer-menards/">Menards sealer guide</a> covers what Midwest shoppers will find there.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Grabbing an asphalt pail for a concrete driveway</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Black emulsion sealers are designed for asphalt only. On concrete they never bond properly and peel within a season, leaving a patchy black mess that is harder to remove than it was to apply. Confirm your surface before you shop, and use a penetrating or acrylic sealer on concrete instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the exact calculated pail count</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rough asphalt absorbs more product than the label average, and running out halfway through a coat leaves a visible lap line and a wet driveway while you drive to the store. Buy one spare pail and return it unopened if you do not need it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving pails in the truck or a cold garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Emulsion sealers are ruined if they freeze, and a pail baked in a hot truck bed can skin over at the top. Store pails indoors at moderate temperatures, off the concrete floor, and use them within the season you bought them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the oil-spot primer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Emulsion will not bond to oil-soaked asphalt, so a stain that is still tacky after scrubbing becomes a bald patch within weeks of sealing. A small can of primer costs little next to the pails, and it is the difference between an even finish and a spotty one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the first weekend of spring</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The first warm Saturday brings everyone to the aisle, but nights may still drop below 50 degrees, which slows curing badly. Wait for a stretch where both day and night temperatures stay mild, and check the hourly forecast for rain on the morning you start.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which Home Depot pail should most blacktop owners pick?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a typical blacktop driveway, Latex-ite Airport Grade is the best all-round choice most stores carry: a filler sealer with a gas and oil resistance claim and a good finish. If you want the longest service life and do not mind a higher price, BLACK JACK Drive-Maxx 1000 is the premium option. For concrete, choose Foundation Armor SX5000 or BEHR Wet-Look depending on the finish you want.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will one pail cover my asphalt drive and concrete apron?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A few products are sold as dual-surface, but none do both jobs as well as a dedicated product. Asphalt needs binder replacement and a black protective film; concrete needs water repellency or a clear acrylic coat. If your property has both surfaces, buy the right product for each. The cost difference is small compared with redoing a failed job.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to seal a driveway with Home Depot products?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a 600 square foot two-car driveway with two coats, expect roughly four or five pails of filler sealer plus a squeegee, brush, crack filler and cleaner. Depending on the tier you choose, that is commonly in the low to mid hundreds of dollars at the time of writing. Our full cost guide compares that with contractor quotes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I find sealer on the shelf in November or February?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not in stores. Sealer is a seasonal product, stocked heavily from early spring through summer and thinned out by late fall in colder regions. Online availability continues longer for some products, and penetrating concrete sealers are often sold online year round. Buy before you need it if you plan to seal in early fall.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I return unused pails of driveway sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Unopened pails are generally returnable with a receipt within the store's standard return window, which is why buying one spare pail is a good habit. Opened or partially used pails are a different matter, so do not open the spare until you are sure you need it. Check the current return policy, as terms can change.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many years should a coat from these pails hold up?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                That depends far more on prep, weather and traffic than on the store. On asphalt, mid-range filler sealers commonly go two to three years between coats, and premium polymer pails may stretch a bit longer. Penetrating concrete sealers often last several years, while acrylic wet-look films usually need recoating every couple of years on a driveway.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy driveway sealer online or in the store?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Check online first either way. If your store shows enough pails, reserve them with in-store pickup so they are held for you. For large orders or online-only products, delivery can save a heavy haul. Buying in person lets you check that pails are undamaged and recently stocked, which matters because old, settled sealer is harder to mix.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

